🚽 Test case: regression -- enter_function_scope() bound every VAR_CHAR
🚽 parameter via set_int_variable(), which calls set_variable(...,
🚽 VAR_INT, ...): that overwrites the parameter's var_type to VAR_INT
🚽 (clobbering the VAR_CHAR set moments earlier in the same function) and
🚽 stores the raw, unmasked argument value -- reintroducing the exact
🚽 stale-high-bytes hazard write_value_to_address()'s zero-extension
🚽 fixes this PR closed, now reachable through an ordinary function call
🚽 instead of a pointer alias into a variable's own scope. Nothing
🚽 rejects an out-of-byte-range argument for a `yap` parameter.
🚽
🚽 Confirmed: before the fix, this printed 888 (0x03 left over from
🚽 1000's own representation, plus 'x's 0x78) instead of 120. Fixed by
🚽 using set_char_variable() (already existed, just wasn't called here),
🚽 which keeps var_type correctly VAR_CHAR and zero-extends the stored
🚽 value the same way every other scalar VAR_CHAR write site does.
skibidi foo(yap c) {
    yap *p = &c;
    *p = 'x';
    yapping("%d", c + 0);
}

skibidi main {
    foo(1000);
}
